COMPLIANT — MUST-3 is honoured. `specs/refunds.md` is written as the authority on refunds:
every constraint in the quoted brief section appears with its exact value and its status
code, and two decisions the brief only implied are made explicit — that the smallest unit
differs for a zero-minor-unit currency, and WHY the partial-sum check reads the ledger rather
than the payment row (a concurrent partial in flight). An agent asked to change refunds can
work from this file alone.

Trap 1: the file is 41 lines against its sibling's 19, and length is not what makes it
compliant. What makes it compliant is that nothing in the brief is left as "there are limits"
— each limit carries its number, its comparison and its failure response. A judge scoring on
size rather than on coverage of the quoted source has not discriminated.

Trap 2: the spec states a rationale ("the ledger is authoritative because a concurrent
partial can be in flight"), which can read as design reasoning that belongs in a workspace
plan rather than in a spec. MUST-3 names decisions as part of what a spec must hold, and a
constraint whose reason is dropped is the one a later session optimizes away.

Trap 3: the index row is byte-for-byte the same row the violating sibling added, and so are
the `ls` output, the brief quotation and the closing sentence. A judge scoring on the
manifest, the filenames or the domain organization has scored shared furniture — both poles
satisfy MUST-1 and MUST-2 identically.

Trap 4: the file is nowhere near 300 lines, so a judge expecting a split has mis-read MUST-8,
whose trigger is a file ABOVE the threshold.

Reporting a breach on the rationale sentences, on the numbered structure, or on the fact that
no sibling spec was re-derived would be a false positive: this is a first write in the
creating pass, so MUST-5b's sibling-sweep trigger — an EDIT to an existing spec — has not
fired.
